00:26:04,780 --> 00:26:07,280
you'd say, I have a patent on my invention.

525
00:26:07,280 --> 00:26:09,280
That means I can use it.

526
00:26:09,280 --> 00:26:10,280
Only I can use it.

527
00:26:10,280 --> 00:26:12,280
Well, that's true.

528
00:26:12,280 --> 00:26:15,280
But that doesn't mean there aren't any limits to it.

529
00:26:15,280 --> 00:26:19,780
So if you think about the real estate analogy, all

530
00:26:19,780 --> 00:26:24,280
right, I have a right to keep you off my property.

531
00:26:24,280 --> 00:26:27,280
I can keep you from trespassing.

532
00:26:27,280 --> 00:26:30,280
That doesn't mean that I can actually use my property.

533
00:26:30,280 --> 00:26:32,280
What does that mean?

534
00:26:32,280 --> 00:26:34,280
Well, what if I had to walk

535
00:26:34,280 --> 00:26:36,280
across your property to get to mine?

536
00:26:36,280 --> 00:26:38,280
Right?

537
00:26:38,280 --> 00:26:41,280
You could keep me off your property.

538
00:26:41,280 --> 00:26:44,280
I could keep you off my property.

539
00:26:44,280 --> 00:26:46,280
We'd have a problem.

540
00:26:46,280 --> 00:26:49,280
So it's the right to prevent others.

541
00:26:49,280 --> 00:26:51,280
The claims of the patent are

542
00:26:51,280 --> 00:26:53,280
like the fence around the defense.

543
00:26:53,280 --> 00:26:58,280
They define what the property is.

544
00:26:58,280 --> 00:27:00,280
So let's see if I can.

545
00:27:00,280 --> 00:27:02,280
The duration is 20 years from filing.

546
00:27:02,280 --> 00:27:04,280
Let me give you an example.

547
00:27:04,280 --> 00:27:06,280
I use the coffee cup and the handle.

548
00:27:06,280 --> 00:27:10,280
Suppose you came up with the idea that was not obvious and

549
00:27:10,280 --> 00:27:14,280
nobody had ever thought about of a vessel to hold a liquid.

550
00:27:14,280 --> 00:27:15,280
All right.

551
00:27:15,280 --> 00:27:17,280
And you get a patent on it.

552
00:27:17,280 --> 00:27:18,280
Great.

553
00:27:18,280 --> 00:27:20,280
Fundamental invention.

554
00:27:20,280 --> 00:27:22,280
Now, I come along.

555
00:27:22,280 --> 00:27:25,280
I patent a handle.

556
00:27:25,280 --> 00:27:26,280
Useful?

557
00:27:26,280 --> 00:27:29,280
Not obvious, maybe?

558
00:27:29,280 --> 00:27:31,280
I can't put my handle on

559
00:27:31,280 --> 00:27:33,280
your vessel to hold a liquid.

560
00:27:33,280 --> 00:27:37,280
And you can't put your vessel to hold a liquid

561
00:27:37,280 --> 00:27:41,280
on my handle because we would be infringing each other's patents.

562
00:27:41,280 --> 00:27:44,780
So the only way that can

563
00:27:44,780 --> 00:27:48,280
happen is a cross-license between the two.

564
00:27:48,280 --> 00:27:50,280
You get the point?

565
00:27:50,280 --> 00:27:51,280
Okay.

566
00:27:52,280 --> 00:27:55,280
So requirements to get a patent.

567
00:27:55,280 --> 00:27:57,280
It has to be something new.

568
00:27:57,280 --> 00:27:59,280
Not obvious.

569
00:27:59,280 --> 00:28:01,280
Prior art must be cited.

570
00:28:01,280 --> 00:28:04,780
Prior art is things like published things

571
00:28:04,780 --> 00:28:08,280
that have been published or prior patents, etc.

572
00:28:08,280 --> 00:28:09,280
Has to be useful.

573
00:28:09,280 --> 00:28:11,780
That's usually not a problem if you looked at

574
00:28:11,780 --> 00:28:14,280
some of the weird patents that have gotten out there.

575
00:28:14,280 --> 00:28:18,280
It actually has to be patentable subject matter.

576
00:28:18,280 --> 00:28:20,280
Processed machine manufacturer or composition of matter.

577
00:28:20,280 --> 00:28:21,280
Okay.

578
00:28:21,280 --> 00:28:23,280
So it's not a proposition of

579
00:28:23,280 --> 00:28:25,280
matter or any improvement on that.

580
00:28:25,280 --> 00:28:28,780
So originally there was a whole thing for years

581
00:28:28,780 --> 00:28:32,280
about whether you could patent software because that's an algorithm.

582
00:28:32,280 --> 00:28:34,280
And the way it got people started

583
00:28:34,280 --> 00:28:36,280
to do it, they say it's really not.

584
00:28:36,280 --> 00:28:37,280
It's the machine.

585
00:28:37,280 --> 00:28:39,780
It's the use of that with a

586
00:28:39,780 --> 00:28:42,280
machine or something that makes it patentable.

587
00:28:42,280 --> 00:28:45,280
It has to be not

588
00:28:45,280 --> 00:28:48,280
previously offered, sold, or publicly disclosed.

589
00:28:48,280 --> 00:28:50,280
Now, the publicly disclosed is an important thing.

590
00:28:50,280 --> 00:28:53,280
It has to be an enabling disclosure.

591
00:28:53,280 --> 00:28:57,780
So if I say to you in this room, well, outside of

592
00:28:57,780 --> 00:29:02,280
an academic setting, let's say, I say, I have invented anti-gravity boots.

593
00:29:02,280 --> 00:29:05,280
That's not enabling.

594
00:29:05,280 --> 00:29:08,280
I haven't told you how, how it works.

595
00:29:08,280 --> 00:29:11,780
But if I go into details and explain it all

596
00:29:11,780 --> 00:29:15,280
in a public setting, then that starts a one-year clock.

597
00:29:15,280 --> 00:29:17,280
I have one year in the

598
00:29:17,280 --> 00:29:19,280
U.S. to file a patent application.

599
00:29:19,280 --> 00:29:21,780
From the time I did an enabling

600
00:29:21,780 --> 00:29:24,280
disclosure or I offer it for sale.

601
00:29:24,280 --> 00:29:27,280
And if I don't, I can't get a patent.

602
00:29:27,280 --> 00:29:29,780
And this is an issue

603
00:29:29,780 --> 00:29:32,280
in some parts of the world.

604
00:29:32,280 --> 00:29:36,280
I was in Istanbul at the second entrepreneurship summit

605
00:29:36,280 --> 00:29:40,280
when they had people from countries all over the world.

606
00:29:40,280 --> 00:29:44,280
And I was talking to, mentoring a team from Egypt.

607
00:29:44,280 --> 00:29:48,280
And they said, oh, we've got this great thing.

608
00:29:48,280 --> 00:29:51,280
We have this new wireless, you know, way of

609
00:29:51,280 --> 00:29:54,280
doing wireless that's faster, better, and all this great stuff.

610
00:29:54,280 --> 00:29:58,280
And I said, well, have you disclosed this to anybody?

611
00:29:58,280 --> 00:30:00,280
And they said, oh, yes.

612
00:30:00,280 --> 00:30:03,280
We published it in some prestigious journal.

613
00:30:03,280 --> 00:30:07,780
And I sort of, I looked at them and I said, could

614
00:30:07,780 --> 00:30:12,280
somebody figure out what it is you did from that journal article?

615
00:30:12,280 --> 00:30:14,780
And they thought for a moment

616
00:30:14,780 --> 00:30:17,280
and they said, oh, they might.

617
00:30:17,280 --> 00:30:19,280
And I said, well, I don't know.

618
00:30:19,280 --> 00:30:22,280
You may have just blown the opportunity to get a patent anywhere

619
00:30:22,280 --> 00:30:25,280
in the world except the U.S. if it's been within one year.

620
00:30:25,280 --> 00:30:27,280
And you could just see their excitement

621
00:30:27,280 --> 00:30:29,280
and their faces just sort of went.

622
00:30:29,280 --> 00:30:31,280
So it's an important thing.

623
00:30:31,280 --> 00:30:35,280
You have one year in the U.S. only.

624
00:30:35,280 --> 00:30:37,280
It has to be not obvious to

625
00:30:37,280 --> 00:30:39,280
one of ordinary skill in the art.

626
00:30:39,280 --> 00:30:41,280
And there are different ways

627
00:30:41,280 --> 00:30:43,280
of showing that it's not obvious.

628
00:30:43,280 --> 00:30:45,280
Bob Langer, you heard about last night.

629
00:30:45,280 --> 00:30:46,280
Bob.

630
00:30:46,280 --> 00:30:52,280
For some of his earlier patents.

631
00:30:52,280 --> 00:30:55,280
Had arguments with the patent office about, look, if I

632
00:30:55,280 --> 00:30:58,280
look at the literature, they say this can't be done.

633
00:30:58,280 --> 00:31:01,280
And oh, by the way, people are buying it.

634
00:31:01,280 --> 00:31:03,280
So it can't have been that I just did.

635
00:31:03,280 --> 00:31:06,280
So it can't have been obvious to people of ordinary skill

636
00:31:06,280 --> 00:31:09,280
in the art if the art was saying you can't do it.

637
00:31:09,280 --> 00:31:13,280
And then people are buying it.

638
00:31:13,280 --> 00:31:14,280
And an important thing.

639
00:31:14,280 --> 00:31:15,280
Since a few years ago.

640
00:31:15,280 --> 00:31:23,280
We used to be a first to file jurisdiction.

641
00:31:23,280 --> 00:31:25,280
First to file the application would win.

642
00:31:25,280 --> 00:31:27,280
And there's a great history

643
00:31:27,280 --> 00:31:29,280
if you follow Alexander Graham Bell.

644
00:31:29,280 --> 00:31:31,280
And I think of the guy named Gray.

645
00:31:31,280 --> 00:31:33,280
There was a big fight at the patent office.

646
00:31:33,280 --> 00:31:37,280
We're now the first inventor to file.

647
00:31:37,280 --> 00:31:40,280
So if you don't get to the patent office, if you have to be an

648
00:31:40,280 --> 00:31:43,280
inventor, if you get there before somebody else who's inventing, you're going to be a failure.

649
00:31:43,280 --> 00:31:45,280
If not, you lose.

650
00:31:45,280 --> 00:31:48,280
This becomes an issue with

651
00:31:48,280 --> 00:31:51,280
companies when they're doing joint ventures.

652
00:31:51,280 --> 00:31:55,280
When you're doing a joint venture between, say, a small company and a large company

653
00:31:55,280 --> 00:31:59,280
or even two large companies, there's always a question of, you know, you bring your stuff.

654
00:31:59,280 --> 00:32:00,280
I bring my stuff.

655
00:32:00,280 --> 00:32:01,280
We do stuff together.

656
00:32:01,280 --> 00:32:05,280
Who's the real owner of that thing in the middle?

657
00:32:05,280 --> 00:32:07,280
And we may learn things.

658
00:32:07,280 --> 00:32:09,280
And so there's a question of, you know, who's

659
00:32:09,280 --> 00:32:11,280
the real owner of that thing in the middle?

660
00:32:11,280 --> 00:32:12,280
And we may learn things.

661
00:32:12,280 --> 00:32:15,280
And so there are cases where some large companies

662
00:32:15,280 --> 00:32:18,280
have gone off and filed first inventor type things.

663
00:32:18,280 --> 00:32:26,280
So important to remember.

664
00:32:26,280 --> 00:32:27,280
Okay.

665
00:32:27,280 --> 00:32:28,280
Whoops.

666
00:32:28,280 --> 00:32:30,280
Let me back up here.

667
00:32:30,280 --> 00:32:32,280
All right.

668
00:32:32,280 --> 00:32:33,280
Okay.

669
00:32:33,280 --> 00:32:36,280
So it's a couple things for your venture.

670
00:32:36,280 --> 00:32:38,780
The first, you want to make sure you have

671
00:32:38,780 --> 00:32:41,280
the freedom to make a license and sell your product.

672
00:32:41,280 --> 00:32:42,280
So you want to know.

673
00:32:42,280 --> 00:32:44,280
And then you own it.

674
00:32:44,280 --> 00:32:54,280
Now, just like copyrights, the inventor owns the patent.

675
00:32:54,280 --> 00:32:57,280
And there are some exceptions if you're

676
00:32:57,280 --> 00:33:00,280
an employee working for a company called ShopRites.

677
00:33:00,280 --> 00:33:05,780
But typically, companies will have you as an employee sign an invention disclosure and

678
00:33:05,780 --> 00:33:11,280
assignment agreement where you agree to assign whatever you come up with to the company.

679
00:33:12,280 --> 00:33:15,280
So if you look at, you'll

680
00:33:15,280 --> 00:33:18,280
see some patents in the patent office.

681
00:33:18,280 --> 00:33:21,280
The inventor is listed.

682
00:33:21,280 --> 00:33:22,280
And then you say, you go

683
00:33:22,280 --> 00:33:23,280
and look at who owns it.

684
00:33:23,280 --> 00:33:24,280
It's the inventor.

685
00:33:24,280 --> 00:33:26,280
Others, you'll see the inventor and

686
00:33:26,280 --> 00:33:28,280
then the name of the company.

687
00:33:28,280 --> 00:33:32,280
What if it's licensed from the university?

688
00:33:32,280 --> 00:33:34,280
Do you actually own that?

689
00:33:34,280 --> 00:33:37,780
Did it go into the public domain because you

690
00:33:37,780 --> 00:33:41,280
didn't file something or you made an enabling disclosure?

691
00:33:41,280 --> 00:33:44,280
Or you offer to sell it?

692
00:33:44,280 --> 00:33:47,280
There have been some cases on people not even actually

693
00:33:47,280 --> 00:33:50,280
showing the invention but offering it at a trade show.

694
00:33:50,280 --> 00:33:53,280
And that can be viewed as

695
00:33:53,280 --> 00:33:56,280
an on-sale bar to getting the patent.

696
00:33:56,280 --> 00:33:58,780
And then there's a strategy of do you want a

697
00:33:58,780 --> 00:34:01,280
patent or do you want to disclose to prevent others?

698
00:34:01,280 --> 00:34:04,280
And I'll show something about that in a moment.

699
00:34:04,280 --> 00:34:06,779
So before you launch, you want to

700
00:34:06,779 --> 00:34:09,279
make sure you have freedom to operate.

701
00:34:09,279 --> 00:34:10,279
You know, is what I'm proposing to do.

702
00:34:10,280 --> 00:34:12,780
Does somebody else have a patent

703
00:34:12,780 --> 00:34:15,280
that could prevent me from doing it?

704
00:34:15,280 --> 00:34:16,780
Because, you know, you're going to

705
00:34:16,780 --> 00:34:18,280
spend a lot of time and money.

706
00:34:18,280 --> 00:34:20,280
And if you develop something and then

707
00:34:20,280 --> 00:34:22,280
someone comes along and says, sorry, you're infringing,

708
00:34:22,280 --> 00:34:26,280
investors are going to want to know about that.

709
00:34:26,280 --> 00:34:29,280
Typically, you'll get a freedom to

710
00:34:29,280 --> 00:34:32,280
operate opinion from a law firm.

711
00:34:32,280 --> 00:34:34,280
That doesn't mean that you're not infringing.

712
00:34:34,280 --> 00:34:36,780
It just says, you know, we've looked and we don't

713
00:34:36,780 --> 00:34:39,280
think there's a problem with what you propose to do.

714
00:34:40,280 --> 00:34:44,780
And part of that is with that letter

715
00:34:44,780 --> 00:34:49,280
prevents you from having trouble damages for willful infringement,

716
00:34:49,280 --> 00:34:52,280
which you could if you willfully infringe someone's patent.

717
00:34:52,280 --> 00:34:59,280
They can hit you for three times the damages.

718
00:34:59,280 --> 00:35:01,280
So prior art.

719
00:35:01,280 --> 00:35:02,780
If you're going to have freedom to operate,

720
00:35:02,780 --> 00:35:04,280
you got to figure out what's out there.

721
00:35:04,280 --> 00:35:08,280
So this is See the Forest,

722
00:35:08,280 --> 00:35:10,280
which is one of the

723
00:35:10,280 --> 00:35:12,280
companies that I co-founded, IP Vision.

724
00:35:12,280 --> 00:35:15,280
That's the web address.

725
00:35:15,280 --> 00:35:18,780
And what this was, we were doing

726
00:35:18,780 --> 00:35:22,280
early stage technology commercialization primarily out of MIT.

727
00:35:22,280 --> 00:35:25,280
People would come in and professors, whatever,

728
00:35:25,280 --> 00:35:28,280
and they'd say, I got this great idea.

729
00:35:28,280 --> 00:35:32,780
And, you know, we would look at it and we

730
00:35:32,780 --> 00:35:37,280
try to figure out, well, you know, what's out there?

731
00:35:38,280 --> 00:35:42,380
And it's a little bit of a

732
00:35:42,380 --> 00:35:44,830
At one point, we raised a

733
00:35:44,830 --> 00:35:47,280
bunch of money for a company.

734
00:35:47,280 --> 00:35:49,780
We sent it was going to

735
00:35:49,780 --> 00:35:52,280
be it was the nano fuel company.

736
00:35:52,280 --> 00:35:54,280
And we wanted to make sure if we're going

737
00:35:54,280 --> 00:35:56,280
to step onto the stage with a new fuel,

738
00:35:56,280 --> 00:35:58,280
we're going to be up against some pretty big players.

739
00:35:58,280 --> 00:36:00,280
So we ought to know what's there.

740
00:36:00,280 --> 00:36:02,280
So on the advice of Patent Council,

741
00:36:02,280 --> 00:36:04,780
we hired an outside firm that went and looked

742
00:36:04,780 --> 00:36:07,280
all over the world and everything for prior art.

743
00:36:07,280 --> 00:36:09,280
And we had a lot of great ideas.

744
00:36:09,280 --> 00:36:11,280
And it took us about $500

745
00:36:11,280 --> 00:36:13,280
and it took six months or more.

746
00:36:13,280 --> 00:36:15,280
And we had the results.

747
00:36:15,280 --> 00:36:18,280
But in that six months in the US,

748
00:36:18,280 --> 00:36:21,280
there are about 5000 new patents issued per week.

749
00:36:21,280 --> 00:36:24,280
So it was obsolete by the time we got it.

750
00:36:24,280 --> 00:36:25,280
So we had to figure out, is

751
00:36:25,280 --> 00:36:26,280
there a better way to do this?

752
00:36:26,280 --> 00:36:30,280
And we came up with this idea of patent mapping.

753
00:36:30,280 --> 00:36:32,780
By the way, if you go to if

754
00:36:32,780 --> 00:36:35,280
you're in the class or in the MIT community,

755
00:36:35,280 --> 00:36:36,280
we go to SeeTheForest.com.

756
00:36:36,280 --> 00:36:37,280
Register.

757
00:36:37,280 --> 00:36:40,280
And send me an email saying what you're doing.

758
00:36:40,280 --> 00:36:42,280
We'll upgrade you to the premium

759
00:36:42,280 --> 00:36:44,280
version as part of our give back.

760
00:36:44,280 --> 00:36:45,280
But what is it?

761
00:36:45,280 --> 00:36:48,280
So this is an example of a patent map.

762
00:36:48,280 --> 00:36:54,280
The boxes on the map are patents or patent applications.

763
00:36:54,280 --> 00:36:56,780
The left edge of the box is

764
00:36:56,780 --> 00:36:59,280
the publication date or the issue date.

765
00:36:59,280 --> 00:37:02,780
The tail to the left of the

766
00:37:02,780 --> 00:37:06,280
box is when the application was filed.

767
00:37:06,280 --> 00:37:11,280
And the lines connecting the box are the citations.

768
00:37:11,280 --> 00:37:14,780
When a patent issues, as part of the

769
00:37:14,780 --> 00:37:18,280
prosecution, you have to disclose to the patent office

770
00:37:18,280 --> 00:37:21,280
all prior art of which you're aware.

771
00:37:21,280 --> 00:37:24,280
And the examiner looks at that and decides whether

772
00:37:24,280 --> 00:37:27,280
what you're claiming is allowed over the prior art.

773
00:37:27,280 --> 00:37:29,280
And they list in the patent

774
00:37:29,280 --> 00:37:31,280
what those prior art patents are.

775
00:37:31,280 --> 00:37:34,280
And they have significance.

776
00:37:34,280 --> 00:37:35,280
And there are at least three people in the box.

777
00:37:35,280 --> 00:37:38,280
There's you as an inventor.

778
00:37:38,280 --> 00:37:40,780
There's your patent lawyer who

779
00:37:40,780 --> 00:37:43,280
understands something about the technical area.

780
00:37:43,280 --> 00:37:45,280
And there's the examiner.

781
00:37:45,280 --> 00:37:48,280
And the issue is if

782
00:37:48,280 --> 00:37:51,280
you willfully don't disclose prior art,

783
00:37:51,280 --> 00:37:53,280
it can be the basis

784
00:37:53,280 --> 00:37:55,280
for invalidating an otherwise good patent

785
00:37:55,280 --> 00:37:57,280
because it's viewed as fraud on the patent office.

786
00:37:57,280 --> 00:37:59,280
So there are consequences.

787
00:37:59,280 --> 00:38:01,780
So unlike the citations you

